### What is a sealed class or interface?

A sealed type restricts which other types may directly extend or implement it. You declare it with the `sealed` modifier and a `permits` clause listing the allowed subtypes. Any other type trying to extend it is a compile error.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public sealed interface Account
        permits SavingsAccount, CurrentAccount, LoanAccount {
    String id();
    BigDecimal balance();
}
```

Only `SavingsAccount`, `CurrentAccount`, and `LoanAccount` may implement `Account`. The compiler and JVM both enforce this.

### What are the permits clause rules?

The rules are strict. Permitted subtypes must be accessible at compile time, must directly extend or implement the sealed type (not grandchildren), and must actually exist. In a named module, they may be in different packages of the same module. On the classpath, they must be in the same package.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

// All three subtypes in the same package (classpath rule)
public sealed interface Account
        permits SavingsAccount, CurrentAccount, LoanAccount {
    String id();
    BigDecimal balance();
}

final class SavingsAccount implements Account {
    private final String id;
    private final BigDecimal balance;

    SavingsAccount(String id, BigDecimal balance) {
        this.id = id;
        this.balance = balance;
    }

    @Override public String id() { return id; }
    @Override public BigDecimal balance() { return balance; }
}

final class CurrentAccount implements Account {
    private final String id;
    private final BigDecimal balance;

    CurrentAccount(String id, BigDecimal balance) {
        this.id = id;
        this.balance = balance;
    }

    @Override public String id() { return id; }
    @Override public BigDecimal balance() { return balance; }
}

final class LoanAccount implements Account {
    private final String id;
    private final BigDecimal balance;

    LoanAccount(String id, BigDecimal balance) {
        this.id = id;
        this.balance = balance;
    }

    @Override public String id() { return id; }
    @Override public BigDecimal balance() { return balance; }
}
```

### What must each permitted subclass be?

Every permitted subclass must carry exactly one of three modifiers.

| Modifier | Meaning | Use case |
|---|---|---|
| `final` | Cannot be extended further | Concrete leaf product |
| `sealed` | Continues the restriction with its own `permits` | Multi-level closed hierarchy |
| `non-sealed` | Reopens for unrestricted extension | Plugin base or third-party extension point |

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public sealed interface Account
        permits SavingsAccount, LoanAccount, PartnerProduct {
    BigDecimal balance();
}

// final: no further subclassing
final class SavingsAccount implements Account {
    @Override public BigDecimal balance() { return new BigDecimal("500000"); }
}

// sealed: continues the restriction
sealed class LoanAccount implements Account
        permits HomeLoan, CarLoan {
    @Override public BigDecimal balance() { return new BigDecimal("1000000"); }
}
final class HomeLoan extends LoanAccount {}
final class CarLoan extends LoanAccount {}

// non-sealed: reopens the hierarchy
non-sealed class PartnerProduct implements Account {
    @Override public BigDecimal balance() { return BigDecimal.ZERO; }
}
// Anyone can extend PartnerProduct freely
class WalletProduct extends PartnerProduct {}
```

### Can you omit the permits clause?

Yes, when all permitted subtypes are declared in the same source file, the compiler infers the permitted set. This is convenient for small, closed hierarchies.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

// No permits clause: compiler infers from same file
public sealed interface PaymentResult {
    record Success(BigDecimal amount) implements PaymentResult {}
    record Failure(String reason) implements PaymentResult {}
    record Pending(String expectedDate) implements PaymentResult {}
}
```

Records are implicitly `final`, so they satisfy the permitted-subtype rule automatically without writing `final`.

### How does sealing enable exhaustive switch?

Because the compiler knows the complete set of subtypes, it can verify that a `switch` covers every case without a `default` branch. If someone adds a new permitted subtype, every switch that was exhaustive fails to compile.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.PaymentResult;
import com.bank.model.PaymentResult.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class PaymentProcessor {
    public String describe(PaymentResult result) {
        return switch (result) {
            case Success s -> "Transferred " + s.amount() + " INR";
            case Failure f -> "Failed: " + f.reason();
            case Pending p -> "Pending until " + p.expectedDate();
            // No default needed: compiler knows the set is complete
        };
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        PaymentProcessor processor = new PaymentProcessor();
        System.out.println(processor.describe(new Success(new BigDecimal("75000"))));
        System.out.println(processor.describe(new Failure("Insufficient funds")));
    }
}
```

```text
Transferred 75000 INR
Failed: Insufficient funds
```

### What are common mistakes with sealed types?

The most common mistake is forgetting the modifier on a permitted subclass.

```java
// WRONG: missing modifier on permitted subclass
// class SavingsAccount implements Account { } // COMPILE ERROR
// Error: sealed, non-sealed or final modifiers expected

// CORRECT: add final
final class SavingsAccount implements Account {
    @Override public BigDecimal balance() { return BigDecimal.ZERO; }
}
```

### How is sealing recorded in the class file?

The compiler writes the permitted set into a `PermittedSubclasses` attribute. The JVM validates at load time that each class claiming to extend a sealed type is actually in the permitted list. This makes the restriction robust even against bytecode manipulation.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class SealedReflection {
    sealed interface Account permits Savings, Current {}
    final record Savings() implements Account {}
    final record Current() implements Account {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("isSealed: " + Account.class.isSealed());
        Class<?>[] permitted = Account.class.getPermittedSubclasses();
        Arrays.stream(permitted)
                .forEach(c -> System.out.println("  Permitted: " + c.getSimpleName()));
    }
}
```

```text
isSealed: true
  Permitted: Savings
  Permitted: Current
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Sealed types combine with records and pattern matching to form algebraic data types. A sealed interface is the sum type (one of these alternatives), each record is a product type (bundled fields), and exhaustive switch is the safe consumer. In a bank's codebase this models a fixed set of account products or transaction states with compile-time guarantees that every variant is handled.
